.TH tkudhash n 0.1 tkutils
.SH tkutils::tkudhash
.PP
The image\-file front end for the perceptual dHash. It decodes an image with Tk's photo image (plus the \fBImg\fR package for JPEG/TIFF/… when available) and hands the pixels to the Tk\-free core \fBtclutils::tudhash\fR. In other words: \fBtudhash\fR does the maths, \fBtkudhash\fR does the loading.
.PP
Needs Tk (8.6+/9.x). \fBImg\fR is optional — PNG/GIF/PPM work with plain Tk, and \fBImg\fR adds JPEG, TIFF and more.
.SS Commands
.CS
tkudhash::fromFile      path ?-sample N?      ;# -> 16 hex chars
tkudhash::similarFiles  pathA pathB ?maxDist? ;# -> bool (default 10)
tkudhash::distance      a b                   ;# -> 0..64  (from tudhash)
tkudhash::similar       a b ?maxDist?         ;# -> bool    (from tudhash)
.CE
.PP
\fBfromFile\fR returns the 64\-bit dHash of an image file as 16 hex chars. \fBdistance\fR/\fBsimilar\fR are re\-exported from \fBtudhash\fR so a caller needs only this one package.
.SS Example
.CS
package require tkutils::tkudhash
namespace import ::tkutils::tkudhash::*

set h1 [fromFile /share/scans/rechnung-042.png]
set h2 [fromFile /share/scans/rechnung-042-kopie.jpg]
if {[similar $h1 $h2]} { puts "same document (distance [distance $h1 $h2])" }

# or in one step:
similarFiles a.png b.png        ;# -> 1 / 0
.CE
.SS "Speed on large scans"
.PP
\fB\-sample N\fR (default 48) caps the work: the image is first subsampled so its long side is at most ~\fB2*N\fR pixels, and only that small copy is read; the core then box\-averages it to 9×8. The hash is deterministic and, because dHash is scale\-invariant, a 60×45 and a 1200×900 version of the same picture produce the same hash. Raise \fBN\fR for a little more fidelity, lower it for speed.
.SS Errors
.PP
Error code \fB{TKUTILS TKUDHASH <REASON>}\fR:
.TS
l l.
REASON	When
\fBLOAD\fR	the file could not be decoded (unknown format → install \fBImg\fR)
\fBEMPTY\fR	the image has zero width or height
\fBOPTION\fR	unknown option to \fBfromFile\fR
.TE
.SS Testing
.PP
\fBtkudhash.test\fR builds gradient PNGs and checks the hash format, that near\-duplicate images are \fBsimilar\fR, that different images are not, scale invariance, and the load error. It needs a Tk display; run under \fBXvfb\fR (passes on 8.6 and 9.0). The maths itself is covered by \fBtudhash\fR's tests.
